==English==
[[File:Oriole 2.jpg|thumb|a {{vern|Eurasian golden oriole|Eurasian golden '''oriole'''}}  ({{taxfmt|Oriolus oriolus|species}}: {{taxfmt|Oriolidae|family}})]]

===Etymology===
A borrowing from {{bor|en|frm|oriol}}, from {{der|en|fro|oriol}}, from {{der|en|la|aureolus}}, diminutive of {{m|la|aureus||golden}}.  {{dbt|en|aureole}}. First use appears c. 1776 in the writings of {{w|Thomas Pennant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɔː.ɹiːˌəʊl/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-oriole.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɔɹ.iˌoʊl/|a=US}}
* {{homophones|en|aureole}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any of various colourful [[passerine]] [[bird]]s, the [[New World]] orioles from the family {{taxfmt|Icteridae|family}} and the [[Old World]] orioles from the family {{taxfmt|Oriolidae|family}}.
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Emily Holden|url=http://www.theguardian.com/environment/2019/oct/10/bird-species-extinction-north-america-climate-crisis|title=Two-thirds of bird species in North America could vanish in climate crisis|date=2019-10-10|work=The Guardian|passage=Those at risk include the wood thrush, a well-known songbird, and the Baltimore '''oriole''', the mascot of Maryland’s baseball team.}}

